<!--
  `F-158` — the bubble's ground.

  ## Why a rounded rectangle and not an oval

  Because the bubble is **not always a circle.** The shape from the assistant
  overlay on pages 22-24 of the owner's PDF is a circle that stretches into a
  pill to say something and settles back, and `SwipBubbleService.buildBubble`
  builds exactly that: a `WRAP_CONTENT` window whose width is driven by
  whether the label is visible.

  An `<shape android:shape="oval">` would track that by turning into an
  ellipse - a lozenge with curved sides, which is not the shape in the
  reference and reads as a stretched circle rather than a pill. A rectangle
  with a corner radius of exactly half the bubble's height is a true circle
  while it is square and a true pill the moment it is not, with no code
  deciding which.

  **So the 24 dp here is load-bearing and is half of the 48 dp in
  `buildBubble`.** Change one and the other has to move, or the circle stops
  being round.

  ## Why the colours are hard-coded rather than taken from the theme

  Same rule as the camera overlays in `swip_tokens.dart`: this is drawn over an
  arbitrary screen that SWIP does not control and cannot measure. It has to
  carry its own contrast, so it is SWIP's ink ground with a gold hairline
  regardless of the app underneath, the wallpaper, or whether the user is in
  Paper or Foil. A bubble that followed the app theme would be a white circle
  on a white screen exactly when somebody needed to find it.
-->
<shape xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android"
    android:shape="rectangle">

    <solid android:color="@color/swip_bubble_ground" />
    <corners android:radius="24dp" />
    <stroke
        android:width="1dp"
        android:color="@color/swip_bubble_edge" />
</shape>
